          "legendFormat": "upstream_timeout (breaker-counting)"
        },
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "prometheus"
          },
          "refId": "B",
          "expr": "sum(rate(gateway_forward_attempts_total{model=~\"$model\", outcome=\"upstream_first_byte_timeout\"}[$__rate_interval]))",
          "legendFormat": "upstream_first_byte_timeout (breaker-counting)"
        },
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "prometheus"
          },
          "refId": "C",
          "expr": "sum(rate(gateway_forward_attempts_total{model=~\"$model\", outcome=\"stream_idle_timeout\"}[$__rate_interval]))",
          "legendFormat": "stream_idle_timeout (not counted)"
        },
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "prometheus"
          },
          "refId": "D",
          "expr": "sum(rate(gateway_forward_attempts_total{model=~\"$model\", outcome=\"client_write_timeout\"}[$__rate_interval]))",
          "legendFormat": "client_write_timeout (client-side, not counted)"
        }
      ],
      "description": "Which deadline ended the forward, and whether the circuit breaker believes it. upstream_timeout means headers never arrived. upstream_first_byte_timeout means headers arrived and no response byte ever did \u2014 for an SSE backend the headers are written on admission, so that is backend ill health and counts. stream_idle_timeout means it produced tokens and then went quiet mid-response, which is not held against it. client_write_timeout is the consumer refusing to read, which says nothing about the backend."
    },
